Found the 2 tables in each ODT — they're "Messpam" messages (spam messages received by the characters, with emojis). They're structured as table > tr > td > p's. My paragraph extraction DROPPED them (only <p> at top level, or the <p> inside the td got extracted separately... actually my regex `re.findall(r'<p[^>]*>(.*?)</p>')` extracted ALL <p> elements INCLUDING those inside tables — so the messpam text probably got inlined as regular paragraphs without the table wrapper!). Let me check whether the messpam text is currently in the pages as plain paragraphs.

Then implement:
1. Extract the tables from the ODT HTML with their position in the flow.
2. Insert them as styled single-cell tables with WHITE borders (user request) — like a message/notification card on screen: white border, maybe slightly different background, the emojis showing.
3. Remove the duplicated inline paragraphs (the <p> from inside the tables that my earlier extraction scattered).

Approach: rebuild the reading-text block again, this time processing the FULL body sequence (paragraphs AND tables in order). The ODT HTML body: sequence of <p> and <table> elements. I'll parse the body sequentially with a regex that captures both, then:
- <p> → paragraphs (as before, cleaning spans)
- <table> → extract the cell content (its <p>s joined with <br>), render as a styled div/table with white borders.

Table styling (white borders, message-like):
```css
.messpam {
  border: 2px solid #e2e8f0;          /* bordures blanches */
  border-radius: 10px;
  padding: 16px 20px;
  margin: 1.4em 0;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.04);
  text-indent: 0;
  font-size: 0.95em;
  line-height: 1.6;
}
```
Keep the emojis ✓ (they're in the text). The italic parts inside ✓ (the <i> in the tables — the "*Protocole actif...*" bits).

Implementation: rebuild the reading-text of both pages from the ODT HTML sequentially. Let me write the script:
- Parse body: iterate over matches of `<p[^>]*>.*?</p>|<table.*?</table>` in order (finditer with alternation, flags=S).
- For tables: extract inner <p> contents, clean, join with <br />; wrap in `<div class="messpam">`.
- Skip the title/date/underscore paragraphs as before (title = first p, date = second; "_" paragraphs skipped).

Then add the .messpam CSS to both pages. Let me check where the messpam text currently sits in the pages (as scattered paragraphs) — the rebuild will replace the whole reading-text anyway ✓.

