content.text:
The user reports: the newsletter popup overlaps the Google Analytics cookie banner — ugly. Both are bottom-fixed: the cookie banner (#cookie-banner, z-index 200, bottom 18px, centered) and the newsletter popup (.nl-popup, z-index 190, bottom 18px right 18px). They collide visually — the popup slides in at the bottom-right and overlaps the cookie banner which spans the bottom center (width min(680px, 100%-30px)) — on smaller screens especially they overlap.

Fix options:
1. **Don't show both at once**: delay the newsletter popup until the cookie banner is dismissed/accepted (the cookie choice comes first — that's also the RGPD-logical order: consent first, then the newsletter invite).
2. Reposition: cookie banner bottom, popup ABOVE it (popup bottom raised when banner visible).
3. Simply increase the popup's bottom offset when the cookie banner is displayed.

Best UX/RGPD: the newsletter popup should not appear while the cookie banner is open. Implement: in the newsletter timing logic, wait until no cookie banner is visible:
```js
var cookieVisible = function() {
  var b = document.getElementById('cookie-banner');
  return b && b.style.display !== 'none';
};
```
The nl show() (setTimeout 14s or scroll 40%) — before opening, check cookieVisible(); if visible, retry every 2s until dismissed (or listen to the banner removal). Simplest: in the show timer, if cookie banner visible → postpone (retry after 3s, up to some limit).

Also — the user might mean they're seeing it right now in the tour because I force-triggered both at once (I called `b.style.display='flex'` AND `nlOpen()` simultaneously for the tour demo!). That overlap was partly MY doing in the demo... But in real conditions: cookie banner appears on DOMContentLoaded (if no consent), newsletter popup at 14s or 40% scroll — a visitor could scroll to 40% within seconds while the cookie banner is still open → overlap ✓ real issue.

Fix: coordinate the two. Modify the nl script on all 6 pages: before nlOpen, check if the cookie banner is displayed; if so, wait (retry loop). Since the cookie banner disappears when the user clicks (b.remove() — the element is REMOVED), the check `document.getElementById('cookie-banner')` returns null after dismissal ✓ clean check.

Implementation — modify the IIFE timing block in the nl script:
```js
      var shown = false;
      var show = function () {
        if (shown) return;
        var cb = document.getElementById('cookie-banner');
        if (cb && cb.style.display !== 'none') {
          // attendre que le bandeau cookies soit traité
          setTimeout(show, 3000);
          return;
        }
        shown = true;
        setTimeout(nlOpen, 800);
        window.removeEventListener('scroll', show);
      };
```
Replace the existing `var show = function () { if (shown) return; shown = true; ... }`.

Apply to all 6 pages (the nl script is identical FR/EN except strings). Python replace of the timing block. Let me do it.

